options.explanation:
The **point-slope form** comes directly from the definition of slope. We'll use $m$ to represent slope and $(x_1, y_1)$ as one point on the graph of the equation.

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{\purpleE{\text{change in }y}}{\goldE{\text{change in }x}} &=\redE{m}\\\\
\dfrac{\purpleE{y-y_1}}{\goldE{x-x_1}} &= \redE{m}\\\\
\purpleE{y-y_1} &= \redE{m}\goldE{(x-x_1)}
\end{align}$

hints.content:
Let's find the slope between $(\blueD{1},\blueD{3})$ and $(\maroonD{5},\maroonD{1})$:

$\begin{align}
\text{Slope}&=\dfrac{\maroonD{1}-\blueD{3}}{\maroonD{5}-\blueD{1}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{-2}{4}
\\\\
&=-\dfrac12
\end{align}$
